{# A case that needs an act from the lawyer while no term is running on it.

   The two lines in the box are the whole reason this template is not the plain
   heading-and-body one: a blocked case is not a deadline about to expire, so the
   message cannot lean on a date to say what it wants. It has to name the fact that is
   missing and the act that unblocks it, apart from the explanation, or the lawyer has
   to reconstruct both out of a paragraph.

   Laid out as a table with inline styles, like the rest of the emails here: mail
   clients drop most of what a browser understands.

   @var heading string
   @var body    string
   @var missing string the fact the case is waiting on
   @var action  string what the lawyer has to do about it
   @var caseUrl string absolute link into the case #}

{% extends 'emails/_layout.html.twig' %}

{% block content %}
    <h2 style="color:#b45309;margin-bottom:16px;">{{ heading }}</h2>

    <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;">{{ body }}</p>

    <table role="presentation" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="100%" style="margin:20px 0;border-left:4px solid #f59e0b;background:#fffbeb;">
        <tr>
            <td style="padding:14px 16px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#78350f;">
                <div>
                    <strong>{{ 'email.blocked_case.missing_label'|trans }}</strong>
                    {{ missing }}
                </div>
                <div style="margin-top:8px;">
                    <strong>{{ 'email.blocked_case.action_label'|trans }}</strong>
                    {{ action }}
                </div>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p style="text-align:center;margin:32px 0;">
        <a href="{{ caseUrl }}"
           style="display:inline-block;padding:14px 28px;background:#2563eb;color:#ffffff;text-decoration:none;border-radius:6px;font-size:16px;font-weight:bold;">
            {{ 'email.cta.view_case'|trans }}
        </a>
    </p>
{% endblock %}
